# {{.Name}}

A [Warren](https://github.com/MerseniBilel/warren) service.

## Run it

```bash
{{.EnvPrefix}}_NAME={{.Name}} go run ./cmd/{{.Name}}
```

It boots the module graph, starts the outbox relay and the notification
consumer, opens readiness, and drains cleanly on SIGTERM. Without
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_NAME` it fails at boot naming the variable — every error the
framework can detect surfaces at boot, never on request 1.

## Test it

```bash
go test -race ./...
```

`internal/modules/user/module_test.go` boots the whole graph and drives the
handler through `App.Invoke`, then asserts the event reached the broker
through the outbox relay. No Docker, no network, no sleeps.

## What is not here yet

**No HTTP server wired yet.** `warren/transport/http` has shipped — uncomment
the `http.Server(...)` line in `cmd/{{.Name}}/main.go` and add the module to
your go.mod to serve requests. `transport/grpc` has not shipped. Everything else
works: use cases, the module graph, config, persistence, the transactional
outbox, the consumer chain, health.

When `transport/http` ships, uncomment one line in `cmd/{{.Name}}/main.go` —
the controllers are already written and registered.

## Layout

```
cmd/{{.Name}}/            main: the module graph and Run()
internal/config/          the app's Config struct
internal/platform/        broker, outbox, unit of work, relay, health
internal/modules/user/    a feature: domain, application, infrastructure
internal/modules/notification/
                          a second feature, consuming the first's events
```

`internal/modules/<name>/module.go` is the only file permitted to see all
four layers. `domain` imports nothing from the others — that rule is why the
domain can be tested, versioned, and extracted on its own.
